{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was the Count of Tripoli and his mother was the sister of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and King Amalric I of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1153 he was forced to sign a treaty with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus recognizing Byzantine suzerainty over Tripoli. However Raymond III soon broke the treaty and began to raid Byzantine territories.\n\n*The Crusader States and the Fatimid Caliphate*\nIn 1164 Raymond III joined forces with King Amalric I of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire to attack the Fatimid Caliphate. The campaign was successful and the Crusaders were able to capture several important cities including Bilbais and Cairo. However the campaign ultimately ended in failure when the Crusaders were forced to withdraw due to a lack of supplies and the arrival of a Fatimid relief force.\n\nRaymond III also played a key role in the politics of the Crusader States. In 1174 he was one of the leaders of the opposition to King Amalric I's plans to invade Egypt. He also supported the claims of Guy of Lusignan to the throne of Jerusalem.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nIn 1180 Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of Saint-Omer. The marriage was likely arranged to strengthen the alliance between Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263596143573523}, 